Description: pvr: lay out twiddled mip levels as power-of-two block grids
 pvr_image_plane_setup_mip_levels() converts the power-of-two physical extent
 of level 0 to blocks and halves the block count for every further level.
 That is only correct while the block dimensions are powers of two. For a
 block size that is not a power of two the block counts do not match what the
 TPU reads: the physical extent is the texel extent rounded up to a power of
 two, and the block count of that extent, rounded up to a power of two again,
 can be larger than the block count of the image extent rounded up to a power of
 two (65 texels in blocks of 5: 128 texels, 26 blocks, 32; against 13 blocks,
 16). A twiddled level also needs a power-of-two block grid: usclib_twiddle2d()
 (pco/usclib/tex.cl) rounds both dimensions up to a power of two before
 interleaving the coordinates.
 .
 For twiddled images take the block extent of the image extent, round each
 dimension up to a power of two and halve it for every level. Other layouts
 keep the block extent of the physical extent. The trailing levels that are
 added to the layer size stop at the same condition as before (block extent
 1x1x1). For block dimensions 1, 2 and 4, that is all formats the driver
 supports before ASTC, the result is the same as before for every level,
 offset, pitch and layer size; checked offline by comparing both
 computations over extents, block sizes, layouts and level counts.
 .
 On the board, the layout from the physical extent made
 dEQP-VK.texture.compressed.astc_*_npot_mip1* (51x65, level 1) fail for
 exactly the block heights that are not a power of two, while the level 0 and
 power-of-two cases passed; the block extent of the image extent explains all
 block sizes of that run. Offsets of levels above 1 are not covered by these
 tests.
 .
 Only for this downstream stack; not intended for upstream submission.
